<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alberto III Pio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Alberto Pio of Carpi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alberto III Pio was the last Pio lord of Carpi, a humanist pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> who rebuilt the town as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> showpiece with a new cathedral and square. A diplomat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, he was deprived of Carpi by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in the 1520s, after which it passed to the Este, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alberto-iii-pio.jpg" alt="Alberto III Pio" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardino Loschi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardino_Loschi_Alberto_III_Pio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
